{"name":"Break the Ice","hex":"#b2e1ee","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"break the ice","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To start to get to know people to avoid social awkwardness and formality.","To introduce a conversation."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/break_the_ice"}],"entries":[{"title":"Break the Ice (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Break the Ice\" is a song by American singer Britney Spears from her fifth studio album, Blackout (2007). It was released as the third and final single from the album on March 3, 2008, by Jive Records. The song was written by Nate \"Danja\" Hills, Jim Beanz, Keri Hilson and Marcella Araica, while production was handled by Danja and vocal production was handled by Beanz. \"Radar\" was originally planned to be released as the third single, but \"Break the Ice\" was released after it was chosen by a poll on Spears's official website. Musically, \"Break the Ice\" is an electro-R&B song with influences of crunk. The song opens with a choir and features synthesizers. Its lyrics deal with an attraction between two people. \"Break the Ice\" received critical acclaim, with reviewers praising its lyrics, production, Spears' vocal performance and deemed it a strong electronic song from the record."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/f/f6/Britney_Spears_-_Break_the_Ice.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Break_the_Ice_(song)","wikidata":"Q845925"},{"title":"Ice","blocks":[{"text":"Ice is water that is frozen into a solid state, typically forming at or below temperatures of 0 °C, 32 °F, or 273.15 K. It occurs naturally on Earth, on other planets, in Oort cloud objects, and as interstellar ice. As a naturally occurring crystalline inorganic solid with an ordered structure, ice is considered to be a mineral. Depending on the presence of impurities such as particles of soil or bubbles of air, it can appear transparent or a more or less opaque bluish-white color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg/330px-Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice","wikidata":"Q23392"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/0a/BreakTheIceMusicVideo.jpg/250px-BreakTheIceMusicVideo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/0/0a/BreakTheIceMusicVideo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/0a/BreakTheIceMusicVideo.jpg/250px-BreakTheIceMusicVideo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The anime-influenced version of Spears in the music video","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABreakTheIceMusicVideo.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Ice_Ih_Crystal_Lattice.png/500px-Ice_Ih_Crystal_Lattice.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Ice_Ih_Crystal_Lattice.png/1280px-Ice_Ih_Crystal_Lattice.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Ice_Ih_Crystal_Lattice.png/500px-Ice_Ih_Crystal_Lattice.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The three-dimensional crystal structure of H2O ice Ih (c) is composed of bases of H2O ice molecules (b) located on lattice points within the two-dimensional hexagonal space lattice (a).[4][5]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIce_Ih_Crystal_Lattice.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thum